## Refactored with `async/await`

```js
/**
 * Fetches a single URL and returns parsed JSON.
 * Throws on network errors or non-2xx responses.
 */
async function load(url) {
  const response = await fetch(url);

  if (!response.ok) {
    throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}: ${response.statusText} (${url})`);
  }

  return response.json();
}

/**
 * Loads all URLs in parallel, preserving order.
 * Rejects immediately if any single fetch fails (fail-fast, like the original).
 */
async function all(urls) {
  try {
    return await Promise.all(urls.map(load));
  } catch (err) {
    throw new Error(`Batch load failed: ${err.message}`, { cause: err });
  }
}
```

### Usage

```js
try {
  const results = await all([
    "https://api.example.com/users",
    "https://api.example.com/posts",
    "https://api.example.com/comments",
  ]);
  console.log(results); // [usersJSON, postsJSON, commentsJSON]
} catch (err) {
  console.error(err.message);
  console.error(err.cause); // original fetch/HTTP error
}
```

---

### What changed and why

| Original | Refactored | Reason |
|---|---|---|
| `cb(null, j)` / `cb(e)` | `return` / `throw` | Eliminates callback hell; errors propagate naturally |
| Manual counter `n` + `forEach` | `Promise.all(urls.map(load))` | Built-in parallelism **with order preservation**
